### How to obtain the values you need for the rotation spreadsheet

If the production deployment *does* inject secrets via environment variables (as is common), they will exist only in the runtime environment, not in the source tree. You can retrieve them in a safe, non‑exposing way with a small helper script that prints only a redacted preview:

```bash
#!/usr/bin/env node
// scripts/redact-secrets.js
// Usage:  node scripts/redact-secrets.js DB_PASSWORD JWT_SECRET API_KEY

const vars = process.argv.slice(2);
if (vars.length === 0) {
  console.error('Provide one or more environment‑variable names as arguments.');
  process.exit(1);
}

vars.forEach(name => {
  const value = process.env[name];
  if (!value) {
    console.warn(`⚠️  ${name} is not set`);
    return;
  }
  const redacted = `${value.slice(0, 4)}${'*'.repeat(Math.max(value.length - 8, 0))}${value.slice(-4)}`;
  console.log(`${name}: ${redacted}`);
});
```

Run it on the machine/container where the app runs (or in a CI job that has access to the same environment) and give it the names of the variables you want to rotate, e.g.:

```bash
node scripts/redact-secrets.js DB_PASSWORD JWT_SECRET API_KEY
```

